1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,624
Drive across a big bridge and at

2
00:00:02,624 --> 00:00:05,166
some point your car goes over a

3
00:00:05,166 --> 00:00:06,724
row of steel teeth,

4
00:00:06,724 --> 00:00:09,922
locked together like the fingers of two

5
00:00:09,922 --> 00:00:10,414
hands.

6
00:00:10,594 --> 00:00:11,964
Thump, thump.

7
00:00:12,144 --> 00:00:14,001
Most people never look down.

8
00:00:14,181 --> 00:00:16,665
But those teeth are there because the

9
00:00:16,665 --> 00:00:17,807
bridge is moving,

10
00:00:17,807 --> 00:00:19,754
right now, under your wheels.

11
00:00:19,934 --> 00:00:22,619
Today, eleven parts of a bridge,

12
00:00:22,619 --> 00:00:24,633
and why each one exists,

13
00:00:24,633 --> 00:00:27,318
starting with those metal teeth.

14
00:00:27,667 --> 00:00:30,233
A bridge looks like the most solid

15
00:00:30,233 --> 00:00:31,592
thing a city owns.

16
00:00:31,772 --> 00:00:32,526
It is not.

17
00:00:32,706 --> 00:00:35,897
It grows and shrinks with the temperature,

18
00:00:35,897 --> 00:00:37,568
it bends under trucks,

19
00:00:37,568 --> 00:00:39,163
it sways in the wind,

20
00:00:39,163 --> 00:00:41,594
and the river under it is slowly

21
00:00:41,594 --> 00:00:43,341
trying to wash it away.

22
00:00:43,521 --> 00:00:45,700
Every part you are about to see

23
00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:48,302
exists to let the bridge move safely,

24
00:00:48,302 --> 00:00:51,114
or to stop something from destroying it.

25
00:00:51,463 --> 00:00:54,356
As always, every fact comes from a

26
00:00:54,356 --> 00:00:56,994
named source, highway agencies,

27
00:00:56,994 --> 00:01:01,078
bridge engineers, and official accident reports,

28
00:01:01,078 --> 00:01:03,631
all listed in the description.

29
00:01:03,811 --> 00:01:05,401
Let's start with the teeth.

30
00:01:05,751 --> 00:01:07,897
Why do bridges have rows of metal

31
00:01:07,897 --> 00:01:09,327
teeth across the road?

32
00:01:09,507 --> 00:01:12,228
Because the bridge changes length with the

33
00:01:12,228 --> 00:01:12,747
weather.

34
00:01:12,927 --> 00:01:16,121
Steel and concrete expand when they get

35
00:01:16,121 --> 00:01:18,987
warm and shrink when they get cold.

36
00:01:19,167 --> 00:01:21,871
A steel deck a hundred meters long

37
00:01:21,871 --> 00:01:25,052
grows by about one point two centimeters

38
00:01:25,052 --> 00:01:27,677
for every ten degrees of warming.

39
00:01:27,857 --> 00:01:30,470
Between a winter night and a summer

40
00:01:30,470 --> 00:01:33,605
afternoon, a long bridge can change length

41
00:01:33,605 --> 00:01:35,322
by tens of centimeters.

42
00:01:35,672 --> 00:01:37,920
If the deck were fixed solidly at

43
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:40,848
both ends, that movement would have nowhere

44
00:01:40,848 --> 00:01:41,257
to go.

45
00:01:41,437 --> 00:01:44,176
In summer, the deck would push against

46
00:01:44,176 --> 00:01:46,554
its supports with enormous force,

47
00:01:46,554 --> 00:01:48,716
and in winter it would pull on

48
00:01:48,716 --> 00:01:49,076
them.

49
00:01:49,256 --> 00:01:51,922
So the bridge is cut into sections

50
00:01:51,922 --> 00:01:54,666
with gaps, called expansion joints.

51
00:01:54,846 --> 00:01:56,647
The teeth, or fingers,

52
00:01:56,647 --> 00:01:57,875
bridge the gap,

53
00:01:57,875 --> 00:02:00,494
sliding in and out of each other

54
00:02:00,494 --> 00:02:03,113
so tires can roll over smoothly.

55
00:02:03,462 --> 00:02:05,396
Look closely on a hot day and

56
00:02:05,396 --> 00:02:07,863
the fingers are pushed deep together.

57
00:02:08,043 --> 00:02:09,512
On a cold morning,

58
00:02:09,512 --> 00:02:10,817
they pull apart.

59
00:02:10,997 --> 00:02:13,981
The bridge is breathing with the seasons.

60
00:02:14,161 --> 00:02:15,458
Under the fingers,

61
00:02:15,458 --> 00:02:18,194
there is usually a trough that catches

62
00:02:18,194 --> 00:02:19,563
rainwater and dirt,

63
00:02:19,563 --> 00:02:21,579
so they do not fall onto the

64
00:02:21,579 --> 00:02:22,660
supports below.

65
00:02:23,009 --> 00:02:26,102
Why do the biggest bridges have joints

66
00:02:26,102 --> 00:02:28,707
made of many steel beams side by

67
00:02:28,707 --> 00:02:30,742
side, instead of fingers?

68
00:02:30,922 --> 00:02:33,626
Because their movements are too large for

69
00:02:33,626 --> 00:02:34,022
teeth.

70
00:02:34,202 --> 00:02:35,880
On a very long bridge,

71
00:02:35,880 --> 00:02:38,169
the deck can move by more than

72
00:02:38,169 --> 00:02:40,076
a meter between extremes.

73
00:02:40,256 --> 00:02:43,319
A single gap that wide would swallow

74
00:02:43,319 --> 00:02:44,935
a motorcycle wheel.

75
00:02:45,115 --> 00:02:48,218
So a modular joint splits the movement

76
00:02:48,218 --> 00:02:49,933
into many small gaps.

77
00:02:50,283 --> 00:02:52,628
Each steel beam sits on its own

78
00:02:52,628 --> 00:02:55,729
supports, with rubber seals between them.

79
00:02:55,909 --> 00:02:59,635
A control system of springs or linkages

80
00:02:59,635 --> 00:03:01,642
keeps the gaps equal,

81
00:03:01,642 --> 00:03:04,317
so each one only opens a few

82
00:03:04,317 --> 00:03:05,464
centimeters.

83
00:03:05,644 --> 00:03:07,962
Ten small gaps can add up to

84
00:03:07,962 --> 00:03:09,784
a huge total movement,

85
00:03:09,784 --> 00:03:12,849
while each one stays narrow enough to

86
00:03:12,849 --> 00:03:14,339
drive over safely.

87
00:03:14,689 --> 00:03:17,434
That rhythmic thump thump you hear when

88
00:03:17,434 --> 00:03:19,827
you drive onto a long bridge often

89
00:03:19,827 --> 00:03:21,516
comes from these joints.

90
00:03:21,696 --> 00:03:24,487
They take a beating from every truck,

91
00:03:24,487 --> 00:03:26,826
and they are among the parts of

92
00:03:26,826 --> 00:03:29,164
a bridge that wear out and need

93
00:03:29,164 --> 00:03:31,125
to be replaced most often,

94
00:03:31,125 --> 00:03:34,444
sometimes while traffic keeps flowing on the

95
00:03:34,444 --> 00:03:35,349
other lanes.

96
00:03:35,700 --> 00:03:38,521
Why does a heavy concrete bridge sit

97
00:03:38,521 --> 00:03:40,088
on blocks of rubber?

98
00:03:40,268 --> 00:03:43,124
Because the deck must be allowed to

99
00:03:43,124 --> 00:03:45,327
move and tilt on top of its

100
00:03:45,327 --> 00:03:46,061
supports.

101
00:03:46,241 --> 00:03:48,898
These bearings are made of layers of

102
00:03:48,898 --> 00:03:51,628
rubber with thin steel plates inside.

103
00:03:51,808 --> 00:03:54,568
The steel stops the rubber from bulging

104
00:03:54,568 --> 00:03:56,054
out under the weight,

105
00:03:56,054 --> 00:03:58,531
so it can carry hundreds of tonnes.

106
00:03:58,711 --> 00:04:01,497
But the rubber can still lean sideways,

107
00:04:01,497 --> 00:04:03,925
letting the deck slide as it grows

108
00:04:03,925 --> 00:04:04,783
and shrinks.

109
00:04:05,133 --> 00:04:07,440
The pads also let the deck rotate

110
00:04:07,440 --> 00:04:08,909
a little at the ends,

111
00:04:08,909 --> 00:04:11,077
as it bends under heavy trucks.

112
00:04:11,257 --> 00:04:13,933
Without them, the corners of the concrete

113
00:04:13,933 --> 00:04:14,717
would crack.

114
00:04:14,897 --> 00:04:16,077
They are simple,

115
00:04:16,077 --> 00:04:18,808
cheap and need almost no maintenance,

116
00:04:18,808 --> 00:04:21,021
which is why you can find them

117
00:04:21,021 --> 00:04:23,604
under thousands of highway bridges.

118
00:04:23,954 --> 00:04:26,419
For bigger loads and movements,

119
00:04:26,419 --> 00:04:28,725
engineers use other bearings.

120
00:04:28,905 --> 00:04:31,685
Pot bearings hold a rubber disc inside

121
00:04:31,685 --> 00:04:32,562
a steel pot.

122
00:04:32,742 --> 00:04:36,475
Sliding bearings use plates of very slippery

123
00:04:36,475 --> 00:04:39,105
plastic against polished steel.

124
00:04:39,285 --> 00:04:42,260
Some bearings in earthquake zones are even

125
00:04:42,260 --> 00:04:44,457
designed to isolate the bridge,

126
00:04:44,457 --> 00:04:46,795
so the ground can shake while the

127
00:04:46,795 --> 00:04:48,282
deck moves much less.

128
00:04:48,633 --> 00:04:51,063
Why do many old steel bridges stand

129
00:04:51,063 --> 00:04:53,632
on curved steel pieces that look like

130
00:04:53,632 --> 00:04:55,715
the bottom of a rocking chair?

131
00:04:55,895 --> 00:04:58,369
Because before modern rubber bearings,

132
00:04:58,369 --> 00:05:00,517
that was how a bridge was allowed

133
00:05:00,517 --> 00:05:01,038
to move.

134
00:05:01,218 --> 00:05:04,020
As the deck grew longer in summer,

135
00:05:04,020 --> 00:05:06,164
the rocker tilted one way.

136
00:05:06,344 --> 00:05:08,689
In winter, it tilted back.

137
00:05:08,869 --> 00:05:12,108
The bridge rocked gently through the seasons.

138
00:05:12,458 --> 00:05:15,197
Inspectors use them like a thermometer.

139
00:05:15,377 --> 00:05:17,709
They know which way a rocker should

140
00:05:17,709 --> 00:05:19,441
lean on a hot or cold day.

141
00:05:19,621 --> 00:05:22,315
If a rocker is tilted too far,

142
00:05:22,315 --> 00:05:25,728
or stuck, it means something is wrong.

143
00:05:25,908 --> 00:05:28,367
Dirt and rust can freeze a rocker

144
00:05:28,367 --> 00:05:31,049
in place, and then the bridge pushes

145
00:05:31,049 --> 00:05:34,104
on its supports instead of moving freely.

146
00:05:34,455 --> 00:05:37,188
Rockers can also tip over in an

147
00:05:37,188 --> 00:05:38,158
earthquake.

148
00:05:38,338 --> 00:05:40,272
In several earthquakes,

149
00:05:40,272 --> 00:05:43,551
decks slid off their bearings and fell.

150
00:05:43,731 --> 00:05:46,821
So many old bridges have been retrofitted

151
00:05:46,821 --> 00:05:49,535
with steel cables or blocks that tie

152
00:05:49,535 --> 00:05:51,420
the deck to its supports,

153
00:05:51,420 --> 00:05:52,550
so it can move,

154
00:05:52,550 --> 00:05:53,832
but not fall off.

155
00:05:54,181 --> 00:05:56,812
Why are there small holes along the

156
00:05:56,812 --> 00:05:58,466
edge of a bridge deck?

157
00:05:58,646 --> 00:05:59,781
They are drains,

158
00:05:59,781 --> 00:06:02,336
and they protect the bridge from its

159
00:06:02,336 --> 00:06:03,684
worst enemy, water.

160
00:06:03,864 --> 00:06:06,162
Rain on a bridge has nowhere to

161
00:06:06,162 --> 00:06:06,755
soak in.

162
00:06:06,935 --> 00:06:08,859
If it stays on the deck,

163
00:06:08,859 --> 00:06:11,904
it makes the road slippery and freezes

164
00:06:11,904 --> 00:06:12,705
in winter.

165
00:06:12,885 --> 00:06:14,851
If it runs down the sides,

166
00:06:14,851 --> 00:06:17,346
it carries salt and dirt onto the

167
00:06:17,346 --> 00:06:19,236
steel and concrete below.

168
00:06:19,585 --> 00:06:22,709
Road salt is especially destructive.

169
00:06:22,889 --> 00:06:25,767
It soaks into the concrete and reaches

170
00:06:25,767 --> 00:06:27,434
the steel bars inside,

171
00:06:27,434 --> 00:06:28,949
which start to rust.

172
00:06:29,129 --> 00:06:32,106
Rust takes up more space than steel,

173
00:06:32,106 --> 00:06:34,917
so it cracks the concrete from the

174
00:06:34,917 --> 00:06:37,233
inside, and pieces fall off.

175
00:06:37,413 --> 00:06:39,880
Much of the damage on older bridges

176
00:06:39,880 --> 00:06:42,488
starts with water that drained in the

177
00:06:42,488 --> 00:06:43,334
wrong place.

178
00:06:43,683 --> 00:06:46,641
So bridge drains are carefully placed,

179
00:06:46,641 --> 00:06:48,898
piped away from the supports,

180
00:06:48,898 --> 00:06:49,988
and inspected.

181
00:06:50,168 --> 00:06:53,469
A blocked drain can quietly destroy a

182
00:06:53,469 --> 00:06:55,253
bridge over decades.

183
00:06:55,433 --> 00:06:56,257
On a bridge,

184
00:06:56,257 --> 00:06:58,937
keeping water moving is as important as

185
00:06:58,937 --> 00:07:00,448
holding up the weight.

186
00:07:00,799 --> 00:07:03,405
Why is the riverbed around a bridge

187
00:07:03,405 --> 00:07:04,746
pier so dangerous?

188
00:07:04,926 --> 00:07:07,201
Because the water is digging it away.

189
00:07:07,381 --> 00:07:09,688
When a river flows around a pier,

190
00:07:09,688 --> 00:07:12,205
the water speeds up and forms swirls

191
00:07:12,205 --> 00:07:14,092
at the front and the sides.

192
00:07:14,272 --> 00:07:17,284
Those swirls lift sand and gravel and

193
00:07:17,284 --> 00:07:18,504
carry them off,

194
00:07:18,504 --> 00:07:21,190
leaving a hole around the base of

195
00:07:21,190 --> 00:07:21,923
the pier.

196
00:07:22,103 --> 00:07:23,907
This is called scour,

197
00:07:23,907 --> 00:07:25,196
and in a flood,

198
00:07:25,196 --> 00:07:26,828
it can happen fast.

199
00:07:27,179 --> 00:07:29,616
In April nineteen eighty seven,

200
00:07:29,616 --> 00:07:31,032
in New York State,

201
00:07:31,032 --> 00:07:34,257
the Schoharie Creek bridge on the Thruway

202
00:07:34,257 --> 00:07:36,223
collapsed during a flood.

203
00:07:36,403 --> 00:07:38,952
Scour had removed the soil under one

204
00:07:38,952 --> 00:07:39,873
of its piers,

205
00:07:39,873 --> 00:07:41,360
and the pier dropped.

206
00:07:41,540 --> 00:07:43,853
Cars and a truck fell into the

207
00:07:43,853 --> 00:07:46,474
river, and ten people were killed.

208
00:07:46,654 --> 00:07:49,201
Scour is one of the leading causes

209
00:07:49,201 --> 00:07:52,123
of bridge failure in the United States.

210
00:07:52,473 --> 00:07:54,797
So piers are now built deeper,

211
00:07:54,797 --> 00:07:57,820
on piles driven far below the riverbed,

212
00:07:57,820 --> 00:08:01,152
and protected with heavy rock placed around

213
00:08:01,152 --> 00:08:01,540
them.

214
00:08:01,720 --> 00:08:05,654
Divers and sonar inspect the riverbed around

215
00:08:05,654 --> 00:08:08,605
bridges, especially after floods.

216
00:08:08,785 --> 00:08:11,121
The most dangerous part of a bridge

217
00:08:11,121 --> 00:08:13,057
may be the part you can never

218
00:08:13,057 --> 00:08:13,324
see.

219
00:08:13,675 --> 00:08:15,890
Why do the cables of many cable

220
00:08:15,890 --> 00:08:18,819
stayed bridges have a thin spiral wrapped

221
00:08:18,819 --> 00:08:19,677
around them?

222
00:08:19,857 --> 00:08:22,307
Because of rain and wind.

223
00:08:22,487 --> 00:08:25,197
When rain runs down a smooth cable

224
00:08:25,197 --> 00:08:26,154
in the wind,

225
00:08:26,154 --> 00:08:29,024
the water forms little rivers on its

226
00:08:29,024 --> 00:08:29,662
surface.

227
00:08:29,842 --> 00:08:32,408
Those rivers change the shape of the

228
00:08:32,408 --> 00:08:33,905
cable in the airflow,

229
00:08:33,905 --> 00:08:36,258
and the cable can start to swing,

230
00:08:36,258 --> 00:08:38,468
sometimes by more than a meter.

231
00:08:38,818 --> 00:08:41,965
This is called rain wind induced vibration,

232
00:08:41,965 --> 00:08:44,673
and it was discovered on real bridges

233
00:08:44,673 --> 00:08:46,503
in the nineteen eighties.

234
00:08:46,683 --> 00:08:48,048
The spiral ridge,

235
00:08:48,048 --> 00:08:51,099
or the dimpled surface on some cables,

236
00:08:51,099 --> 00:08:53,829
breaks up those rivers of water so

237
00:08:53,829 --> 00:08:55,193
they cannot form.

238
00:08:55,373 --> 00:08:58,370
Dampers near the bottom of the cables

239
00:08:58,370 --> 00:09:01,771
also absorb any shaking that still starts.

240
00:09:02,120 --> 00:09:05,159
A swinging cable is not just scary

241
00:09:05,159 --> 00:09:05,963
to watch.

242
00:09:06,143 --> 00:09:08,574
Over years, the bending at the anchors

243
00:09:08,574 --> 00:09:09,789
can tire the steel.

244
00:09:09,969 --> 00:09:12,735
So a cable stayed bridge is tested

245
00:09:12,735 --> 00:09:15,583
in wind tunnels before it is built,

246
00:09:15,583 --> 00:09:18,431
and its cables are designed to stay

247
00:09:18,431 --> 00:09:19,733
calm in a storm.

248
00:09:20,082 --> 00:09:22,840
Why do some suspension bridges pump dry

249
00:09:22,840 --> 00:09:24,750
air into their main cables?

250
00:09:24,930 --> 00:09:27,600
Because the cables rust from the inside.

251
00:09:27,780 --> 00:09:30,456
A main cable is made of thousands

252
00:09:30,456 --> 00:09:33,376
of thin steel wires packed together.

253
00:09:33,556 --> 00:09:36,217
Moisture gets trapped between them,

254
00:09:36,217 --> 00:09:39,333
and the wires slowly corrode where nobody

255
00:09:39,333 --> 00:09:39,941
can see.

256
00:09:40,121 --> 00:09:41,925
On some older bridges,

257
00:09:41,925 --> 00:09:45,613
inspectors opened the cables and found broken

258
00:09:45,613 --> 00:09:47,006
and rusted wires.

259
00:09:47,357 --> 00:09:50,055
So engineers wrap the cable in an

260
00:09:50,055 --> 00:09:53,243
airtight cover and blow dry air through

261
00:09:53,243 --> 00:09:56,105
it, from machines on the towers and

262
00:09:56,105 --> 00:09:57,004
anchorages.

263
00:09:57,184 --> 00:09:59,845
The dry air pushes the moisture out

264
00:09:59,845 --> 00:10:01,898
and keeps the humidity low.

265
00:10:02,078 --> 00:10:05,053
Japan used this method on the Akashi

266
00:10:05,053 --> 00:10:08,442
Kaikyo Bridge, and many bridges in Europe

267
00:10:08,442 --> 00:10:11,335
and the United States have added it

268
00:10:11,335 --> 00:10:11,830
since.

269
00:10:12,180 --> 00:10:15,355
Replacing a main cable on a suspension

270
00:10:15,355 --> 00:10:19,282
bridge is almost impossible without closing the

271
00:10:19,282 --> 00:10:20,702
bridge for years.

272
00:10:20,882 --> 00:10:22,699
So it is much better to keep

273
00:10:22,699 --> 00:10:24,516
the original cables healthy.

274
00:10:24,696 --> 00:10:27,861
A few fans and dehumidifiers protect a

275
00:10:27,861 --> 00:10:29,944
structure worth billions.

276
00:10:30,294 --> 00:10:32,630
Why are the decks of modern long

277
00:10:32,630 --> 00:10:35,622
bridges shaped like a flat airplane wing?

278
00:10:35,802 --> 00:10:38,600
Because of one famous disaster.

279
00:10:38,780 --> 00:10:40,913
In November nineteen forty,

280
00:10:40,913 --> 00:10:44,466
the Tacoma Narrows Bridge in Washington State

281
00:10:44,466 --> 00:10:47,072
twisted itself apart in a wind of

282
00:10:47,072 --> 00:10:49,914
only about sixty kilometers an hour.

283
00:10:50,094 --> 00:10:52,382
Its deck was thin and had solid

284
00:10:52,382 --> 00:10:54,816
sides, and the wind made it twist

285
00:10:54,816 --> 00:10:56,956
more and more until it broke.

286
00:10:57,306 --> 00:11:00,042
Nobody died except a dog left in

287
00:11:00,042 --> 00:11:03,034
a car, but engineers learned a huge

288
00:11:03,034 --> 00:11:03,633
lesson.

289
00:11:03,813 --> 00:11:06,123
Wind does not only push a bridge.

290
00:11:06,303 --> 00:11:08,950
It can make it twist and oscillate.

291
00:11:09,130 --> 00:11:12,305
Since then, long bridges are tested as

292
00:11:12,305 --> 00:11:14,226
models in wind tunnels,

293
00:11:14,226 --> 00:11:17,568
and many have streamlined box decks that

294
00:11:17,568 --> 00:11:20,241
let the air slide past smoothly.

295
00:11:20,592 --> 00:11:22,967
Some bridges use open grids,

296
00:11:22,967 --> 00:11:25,681
slots in the middle of the deck,

297
00:11:25,681 --> 00:11:28,904
or stiff trusses to stop the twisting.

298
00:11:29,084 --> 00:11:32,136
The Tacoma Narrows collapse was filmed,

299
00:11:32,136 --> 00:11:34,562
and that film is still shown to

300
00:11:34,562 --> 00:11:37,536
engineering students around the world.

301
00:11:37,716 --> 00:11:39,797
A bridge does not just have to

302
00:11:39,797 --> 00:11:40,491
be strong.

303
00:11:40,671 --> 00:11:42,041
It has to be calm.

304
00:11:42,391 --> 00:11:44,784
Why do bridges freeze before the road

305
00:11:44,784 --> 00:11:45,560
around them?

306
00:11:45,740 --> 00:11:48,409
Because a bridge has air on both

307
00:11:48,409 --> 00:11:48,910
sides.

308
00:11:49,090 --> 00:11:51,494
A normal road sits on the ground,

309
00:11:51,494 --> 00:11:53,971
which stores heat from the day and

310
00:11:53,971 --> 00:11:56,594
keeps the surface a little warmer at

311
00:11:56,594 --> 00:11:57,031
night.

312
00:11:57,211 --> 00:11:59,975
A bridge deck has cold air above

313
00:11:59,975 --> 00:12:02,134
it and cold air below it,

314
00:12:02,134 --> 00:12:04,898
so it loses heat from both sides

315
00:12:04,898 --> 00:12:07,230
and cools down much faster.

316
00:12:07,581 --> 00:12:09,846
That is why you see signs warning

317
00:12:09,846 --> 00:12:12,387
that the bridge ices before the road.

318
00:12:12,567 --> 00:12:15,193
A driver can come off a wet

319
00:12:15,193 --> 00:12:18,597
but safe road onto a bridge covered

320
00:12:18,597 --> 00:12:20,833
in thin, invisible ice.

321
00:12:21,013 --> 00:12:24,437
Some bridges even have sensors and automatic

322
00:12:24,437 --> 00:12:27,628
sprayers that spread anti icing liquid on

323
00:12:27,628 --> 00:12:30,429
the deck when the temperature drops.

324
00:12:30,779 --> 00:12:33,053
It is a simple bit of physics

325
00:12:33,053 --> 00:12:34,621
with deadly results.

326
00:12:34,801 --> 00:12:38,300
Every winter, accidents happen on bridges while

327
00:12:38,300 --> 00:12:40,235
the roads nearby are fine.

328
00:12:40,415 --> 00:12:42,893
If you remember one thing from this

329
00:12:42,893 --> 00:12:44,734
video, let it be this one.

330
00:12:44,914 --> 00:12:47,654
Slow down on bridges in the cold.

331
00:12:48,004 --> 00:12:50,398
Why does it seem that some steel

332
00:12:50,398 --> 00:12:52,868
bridges are always being painted?

333
00:12:53,048 --> 00:12:56,015
Because paint is the main armor of

334
00:12:56,015 --> 00:12:58,458
a steel bridge against rust.

335
00:12:58,638 --> 00:13:00,826
On Scotland's Forth Bridge,

336
00:13:00,826 --> 00:13:04,228
painting the bridge became a famous saying

337
00:13:04,228 --> 00:13:06,334
for a job that never ends,

338
00:13:06,334 --> 00:13:09,575
because by the time the painters reached

339
00:13:09,575 --> 00:13:12,653
one end, the other end needed painting

340
00:13:12,653 --> 00:13:13,139
again.

341
00:13:13,490 --> 00:13:15,409
In two thousand eleven,

342
00:13:15,409 --> 00:13:17,996
the Forth Bridge got a new kind

343
00:13:17,996 --> 00:13:21,334
of coating, a system first developed for

344
00:13:21,334 --> 00:13:23,253
offshore oil platforms,

345
00:13:23,253 --> 00:13:26,340
expected to last at least twenty five

346
00:13:26,340 --> 00:13:26,841
years.

347
00:13:27,021 --> 00:13:30,134
The painters removed the old layers down

348
00:13:30,134 --> 00:13:32,780
to bare steel and applied new ones

349
00:13:32,780 --> 00:13:35,426
in a big project that lasted about

350
00:13:35,426 --> 00:13:36,204
ten years.

351
00:13:36,555 --> 00:13:39,597
Every steel bridge has a maintenance plan

352
00:13:39,597 --> 00:13:40,339
like this.

353
00:13:40,519 --> 00:13:43,649
Inspectors climb, hang from ropes,

354
00:13:43,649 --> 00:13:46,870
or use drones to check every joint,

355
00:13:46,870 --> 00:13:48,159
bolt and weld.

356
00:13:48,339 --> 00:13:50,800
A bridge is not built once.

357
00:13:50,980 --> 00:13:52,419
It is kept alive,

358
00:13:52,419 --> 00:13:53,773
year after year,

359
00:13:53,773 --> 00:13:56,820
by people most drivers never notice.

360
00:13:57,170 --> 00:13:59,271
So here are the eleven.

361
00:13:59,451 --> 00:14:02,227
Metal teeth that let the bridge breathe,

362
00:14:02,227 --> 00:14:04,587
and joints with many beams for the

363
00:14:04,587 --> 00:14:05,836
biggest movements.

364
00:14:06,016 --> 00:14:08,151
Rubber pads and old rockers,

365
00:14:08,151 --> 00:14:10,438
holes in the deck to drain the

366
00:14:10,438 --> 00:14:13,106
water, and a riverbed that tries to

367
00:14:13,106 --> 00:14:14,631
wash the piers away.

368
00:14:14,811 --> 00:14:18,073
Spiral cables against rain and wind,

369
00:14:18,073 --> 00:14:20,882
dry air inside the main cables,

370
00:14:20,882 --> 00:14:23,237
a deck shaped like a wing,

371
00:14:23,237 --> 00:14:25,865
a surface that freezes first,

372
00:14:25,865 --> 00:14:28,221
and paint that never ends.

373
00:14:28,571 --> 00:14:31,151
Next time you feel that thump thump

374
00:14:31,151 --> 00:14:32,036
on a bridge,

375
00:14:32,036 --> 00:14:32,773
look down.

376
00:14:32,953 --> 00:14:34,627
The bridge is moving,

377
00:14:34,627 --> 00:14:37,017
exactly as it was designed to.

378
00:14:37,197 --> 00:14:40,301
If you want more machines and structures

379
00:14:40,301 --> 00:14:42,086
explained part by part,

380
00:14:42,086 --> 00:14:44,337
subscribe to Machine Reverse.

381
00:14:44,517 --> 00:14:46,321
And tell us in the comments what

382
00:14:46,321 --> 00:14:48,070
you want us to take apart next.
